From 59799c8237a3ef6fbbf50a651f874a3e1a4c9594 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yasuhiro Niji <aiseed-dev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 00:34:10 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] [se] Show dates in the Japanese era with the g, e and r
 format codes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Excel's Japanese date formats name the era with g and its year with e:
[$-411]ggge"年"m"月"d"日" and [$-411]ge.m.d are the ja-jp built-in
formats 27-29, 36 and 50-58 of ECMA-376 Part 1, 18.8.30. g was shown as
a literal letter, and e was read as the start of a scientific format,
which swallowed the next character, so a date came out as
"ggg年m月d日" or "g46301.m.d".

The codes now follow 18.8.31 (International Considerations). In the
ja-jp locale, g is the initial of the era (R), gg its first kanji (令)
and ggg its name (令和); e is the year in the era and ee the same with a
leading zero; r is ee and rr is gggee. The locale is the format's
[$-411], or the workbook's when the format names none. Outside ja-jp, e
is yyyy and ee is yy, as 18.8.31 says, and g shows nothing. E+ and E-
stay scientific.

Excel also writes [$-ja-JP-x-gannen], which names the language with a
tag; it is read as ja-jp. G/標準, the Japanese name of General, was
shown as text and is now General, so the new g does not turn it into
"R/標準".

Assisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5
---
 common/NumFormat.js                           | 101 +++++++++++++++++-
 .../spreadsheet-calculation/NumFormatParse.js |  41 +++++++
 2 files changed, 138 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/common/NumFormat.js b/common/NumFormat.js
